<George_Washington> <people.person.quotations> "I anticipate with pleasing expectations that retreat in which I promise myself to realize, without alloy, the sweet enjoyment of partaking, in the midst of my fellow citizens, the benign influence of good laws under a free government, the ever favorite object of my heart, and the happy reward, as I trust, of our mutual cares, labors, and dangers." .
<His_acceptance_of_Jay's_Treaty_(1795),_which_settled_outstanding_differences_between_the_United_States_and_Britain_(but_which_Democratic-_Republicans_viewed_as_a_surrender_to_British_demands),_revived_attacks_on_the_President.> <base.kwebbase.kwsentence.next_sentence> "By March 1797, when Washington left office, the country's financial system was well established, the American Indian threat east of the Mississippi had been largely eliminated, and both Jay's and Pinckney's Treaty (1795) with Spain had enlarged U.S. territory and removed serious diplomatic difficulties." .
